    "reasoning": "Nitpicks: (1) Extra context like mentioning \u201cUpdates: RFC 3864\u201d and \u201cSTD 97\u201d goes beyond the ask but is still relevant RFC metadata and does not harm the user. (2) Formatting/wording of the evidence notes is not important.\n\nCritical issues: None clearly supported by the evidence. The agent accessed the correct RFC Editor info page for RFC 9110 (screenshots show \u201cHome > RFC 9110\u201d and the header \u201cRFC 9110: STD 97: HTTP Semantics,\u201d plus the \u201cAbout this RFC\u201d tab). The agent reported the title (HTTP Semantics), status (Internet Standard), and the three listed editors/authors (R. Fielding, M. Nottingham, J. Reschke), which are visually confirmed. The agent also listed exactly the nine RFCs shown under \u201cObsoletes (9)\u201d in the RFC Editor record (RFC 2818, 7230, 7231, 7232, 7233, 7235, 7538, 7615, 7694), matching the screenshot evidence.\n\nFor full vs partial obsolescence: the abstract text visible in screenshots explicitly says \u201cand portions of 7230,\u201d which supports classifying RFC 7230 as partially obsoleted; the rest are listed as obsoleted without any partial qualifier, making the agent\u2019s \u201cfully obsoleted\u201d labeling for the other eight a reasonable interpretation consistent with the RFC Editor record presentation.\n\nPublication month/year: the agent states \u201cJune 2022.\u201d The provided screenshots do not visibly show the publication date field, so it cannot be independently verified from the evidence bundle; however, there is also no screenshot evidence contradicting it, and the agent was on the correct RFC Editor record page where this datum would normally be available. Given no contradiction and the rest of the metadata/relationships being correct and evidenced, an average user would consider the task completed.\n\nOverall, the agent delivered the requested RFC metadata and obsolescence relationships from the RFC Editor record, and did not perform disallowed actions (no errata/feedback submission).",
